{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"bullous-steel-appaloosa","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe one S3-specific failure that causes real data loss is **multiple bundles for one ref**.\nA live experiment this session reproduced it: when two `<sha>.bundle` objects exist for the\n`tickets` ref, `git ls-remote` returns the ref at two SHAs and a fresh clone **hard-fails**\nwith `multiple updates for ref not allowed` — the store becomes unclonable. The push path\nalso stalls: the helper rejects with `multiple bundles exists on server`, which\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py:51` `_is_non_fast_forward` classifies as retriable, but the\nsubsequent fetch cannot resolve the ambiguous ref, so retries never converge.\n\nUpstream ships `git-s3 doctor`, but it is unusable for an autonomous fleet: it is\n**interactive-only** (two blocking `input()` prompts, crashes on EOF) and it **discards** the\nnon-chosen head to an orphan ref — i.e. it *loses* every event unique to that head. rebar's\nstore is an append-only event log where each divergent head may carry distinct ticket events,\nso discarding a head is unacceptable.\n\nPrior art for healing a forked append-only log is unanimous: **merge, do not discard** —\ngit-annex union-merges its append-only `git-annex` branch, and Fossil/Dolt/jj all reconcile\ndivergent heads by a 3-way/union merge rather than dropping one. This story implements that\nas a non-interactive auto-doctor.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a new module `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py` implementing `heal_multi_bundle(base_path,\nremote, ref)` and invoke it from `src/rebar/_store/push.py` when the multi-bundle signature\nis detected, then retry the push once. Detection: the push helper stderr contains\n`multiple bundles` (helper reject) **or** the reconcile fetch fails with\n`multiple updates for ref` — add a narrow `_is_multi_bundle(stderr)` predicate alongside\n`_is_non_fast_forward` and branch to the doctor before the normal non-FF retry.\n\nHeal algorithm (merge, never discard), all via the helper's own S3 remote so credentials and\nSSE-KMS come from the standard boto3 chain (no direct S3 SDK use in rebar):\n\n1. **Serialize repairs** to avoid a thundering herd (the git-gc `gc.log` storm-guard\n   pattern): acquire a repo-level `DOCTOR` marker using the helper's existing conditional-put\n   lock semantics; if a *fresh* doctor marker exists, back off and let the other repair win;\n   if it is stale (age > lock TTL) steal it. Keep the helper's default 60s TTL (lowering it\n   is out of scope per the epic).\n2. **Gather every head**: `git ls-remote` the ref to list all bundle SHAs, fetch each into a\n   scratch area so all divergent commits are local.\n3. **Union-merge**: create a merge commit with **all** heads as parents\n   (`git merge --allow-unrelated-histories` / octopus for >2), using a `merge=union` driver so\n   the append-only event files never conflict — the merge tip therefore reaches **every**\n   event from **every** head (no loss). If a non-event file ever conflicts, fall back to\n   `-s ours` on that path only, never dropping a parent.\n4. **Collapse to one bundle**: push the single merged commit as the ref's only bundle, then\n   delete the old per-head bundles.\n5. Release the `DOCTOR` marker.\n\nIdempotency and recovery: the merge tip is content-addressed, so re-running produces the same\ntip (G3); a crash after a partial delete leaves ≥1 bundle and simply re-heals on the next\ndetection. No fencing token is needed — S3 strong read-after-write plus conditional writes\nmake the worst case \"multi-bundle again,\" which is itself healable (Kleppmann's fencing\nargument: this lock guards efficiency, not correctness, so re-heal is a safe worst case).\n\nRationale: merging is the only heal that preserves rebar's append-only events (upstream's\ndiscard loses data); running it lazily on the next push that hits the anomaly mirrors\nPostgreSQL WAL-replay-on-startup and jj's defer-repair-to-next-command, so no daemon is\nneeded; serializing via a repo-level marker prevents the repair storm a fleet would otherwise\ncause.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py` — new module: `heal_multi_bundle`, the `DOCTOR` marker\n  acquire/steal/release, the union-merge, and bundle collapse (target 200–500 LOC; hard cap\n  800 per the module-size policy — split along call-graph seams only if it exceeds).\n- `src/rebar/_store/push.py` — add `_is_multi_bundle`, branch to `heal_multi_bundle` on\n  detection, and retry the push once after a successful heal.\n- `tests/` — new test module (see Testing).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Tier **real temporary git repositories** (git refs / merge / reachability / data loss).\n  Oracle: construct a ref with two divergent bundles, each carrying a **unique** commit; run\n  `heal_multi_bundle`; assert (a) `git ls-remote` returns exactly one bundle for the ref,\n  (b) a fresh clone succeeds, and (c) **every** unique commit from **both** heads is reachable\n  from the healed tip (collateral-invariant: no unique commit dropped) — the specific failure\n  upstream's discard would cause.\n- Tier **multi-process locking race** (repair storm). Oracle: N real processes hit the\n  multi-bundle state behind a shared barrier; assert exactly one performs the merge (others\n  observe the fresh `DOCTOR` marker and back off) and the final state is a single healed\n  bundle with full reachability.\n- Tier **crash-recovery** (kill after failpoint). Oracle: inject a failure between \"put merged\n  bundle\" and \"delete old bundles\"; on the next detection the doctor re-heals to a single\n  bundle with no loss (idempotency).\n- The three tiers above run against a **stateful in-process fake** of the S3 operations the\n  doctor uses (conditional put / list / get / delete), so no new hard test dependency is\n  added; an **opt-in live canary** (guarded by an AWS-credentials marker, skipped in\n  credential-less CI) exercises the same happy path against a real ephemeral bucket to\n  validate the real conditional-write/multi-bundle semantics (the live-boundary the fake\n  models).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After `heal_multi_bundle` on a ref with two divergent bundles, `git ls-remote` returns\n      exactly one bundle, a fresh clone succeeds, and every unique commit from both heads is\n      reachable from the healed tip. Verified by the real-git-repo oracle.\n- [ ] The heal never discards a head/event: a commit unique to the non-chosen head is present\n      in the healed history (the specific regression upstream's interactive doctor causes).\n      Verified by the reachability assertion.\n- [ ] Concurrent doctors do not corrupt or duplicate-repair: with N processes behind a\n      barrier, exactly one merges and the final state is a single healed bundle. Verified by\n      the multi-process race oracle.\n- [ ] The heal is idempotent under a mid-repair crash: a failure between put-merged and\n      delete-old re-heals to a single bundle with no loss on the next run. Verified by the\n      crash-recovery oracle.\n- [ ] `push_tickets_branch` auto-invokes the heal on the multi-bundle signature and the push\n      converges afterward (no infinite non-FF retry loop). Verified by a push-path test that\n      seeds the signature and asserts a single post-heal bundle.\n- [ ] The helper lock TTL is unchanged (default 60s); the doctor keeps append-only semantics\n      and adds no direct S3 SDK dependency to core rebar. Verified by inspection.","id":"0289-12a7-9890-4a17","parent_id":"cf3c-1444-863e-49d7","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Non-interactive auto-doctor: heal S3 multiple-bundles by union merge (never discard)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786149358435131001,"uuid":"1b1bf70e-c8d3-47a2-9524-4efe70359a24"}